WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.417
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There's more
to Google Kubernetes Engine

00:00:02.417 --> 00:00:04.230
cost optimization than
your applications.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.880
There's also your machines.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.520
The underlying virtual
machines are a fundamental part

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.960
of GKE costs.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.750
And choosing the right VMs
to run and fit your apps

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.079
is critical for balancing
cost and performance.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.390
Today, we'll go over some
tips and best practices

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:21.420
for choosing and customizing
your machine types.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.650
Let's go beyond your GKE bill.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.240
Kubernetes does a lot of the
work to abstract the underlying

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.320
infrastructure.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.760
But it's still there.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.190
And how you choose to set it
up can make a big difference

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.032
in how your apps run
and how much they cost.

00:00:41.032 --> 00:00:42.990
In the last video, we
went over the differences

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.550
between understanding
the resources

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.650
that your applications
actually use

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.810
and what they've been allocated.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.060
With that in mind, you can
also adapt the resources

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.280
to best fit your
applications while still

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.678
optimizing for costs.

00:00:54.678 --> 00:00:56.970
I'll start with a review of
the different machine types

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.760
available to GKE and
how to optimize shape

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.250
and size, along with binpacking.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.010
After that, I'll go
over preemptible VMs

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.690
and finish up with
some suggestions

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.820
for cost optimization.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.890
Let's start with some
general machine types.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.900
The N-series machines
were the default

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.060
for most general
workloads on Google Cloud.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.830
And they were a
great combination

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.840
of powerful and flexible.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.120
Not too long ago,
the E-series or E2

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.420
came out as an updated
general purpose machine type.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.280
It's a bit less flexible
than the N-series,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.470
but it's also a bit cheaper.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.780
One example is that
the N-series and E2

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.360
series have different sets of
processors that are available.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.420
Here's a table of the
current differences,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.400
but it's a lot of information.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.100
These differences might be
important for your apps,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.600
but they might not be.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.030
E2s have similar
performance to N1s,

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.040
but they're optimized for cost.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.975
See if they'll work for your
apps and save some money.

00:01:49.975 --> 00:01:51.600
Of course, it's all
about understanding

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.060
the details of your app
when making a decision.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.780
There's also a few other
types like compute optimized

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.460
and memory optimized.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.640
These are, well, optimized
to help run workloads that

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.800
need that extra CPU or memory.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.750
So maybe instead of
spreading your deployments

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.910
across a bunch of
general machines,

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.210
you could stack them onto
fewer optimized machines.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.550
That's where the shape
of your machines come in.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.362
If your apps have
specific requirements,

00:02:16.362 --> 00:02:17.820
you can make sure
that the machines

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.590
are shaped to fit your apps.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.580
You're not just limited to
the predefined configurations.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.730
In fact, all of this
is known as binpacking.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.400
It's all about making sure that
you find the most efficient way

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.140
to run apps on your machines
and to minimize wasted space.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.610
Kubernetes will try to find
a node to schedule your pods.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.160
And if it can't find
one, it'll add more nodes

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.710
according to your node
pool configuration.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.270
Let's take a look at an example.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:46.920
Here we have two machines, the
E2-standard-16 with 16 vCPUS

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.750
and 64 gigs of memory and a
custom E2 with the same vCPUs

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.640
but only 16 gigs of memory.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.910
We also have two applications
with different sizes,

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.620
and app A has two replicas.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.070
So what happens if
Kubernetes needs to schedule

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.320
two more replicas of app B?

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.700
The standard has enough
memory but not enough vCPU.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.910
On the custom, there is
enough vCPU for one but not

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:13.440
enough memory for any.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.810
That means Kubernetes
needs to make more nodes.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.090
And since two replicas
of app B couldn't

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.860
fit on a new
standard, that means

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.400
two more nodes and
even more wasted space.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.940
To help optimize usage, think
about the size of our pods.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.950
They're not all going
to be the same size,

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.980
but when we have a general
sense of each application,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.140
we can optimize
nodes around that.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.450
In fact, one strategy
is to use the 2

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.330
to the power of n rule,
which involves shaping pods

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:44.950
into vCPU buckets of one, two,
four, eight, 16, and so on.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.220
Now we can have a pretty
predictable idea of how

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.810
these will fit on the machines.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:52.330
And we can use larger
machines to better binpack.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.950
This E2-standard-32 can
hold all of our pods.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:59.400
And it's much cheaper than
the four previous machines.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.960
There's still some
wasted memory,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.340
but we could use a custom
size to help with that.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.380
Ultimately, you know your apps.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.780
And the more details you
have, the better decisions

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.370
you can make on machine types.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.040
Plus, you can always try out
different test environments

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.050
and see what happens.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:17.390
Of course, this is still
a lot to think about when

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.190
looking at cost optimization.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:23.000
The good news is that GKE offers
node auto provisioning, which

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.610
automatically figures
out optimal machine sizes

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.730
and then dynamically adds and
removes node pools for you.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:32.120
We'll go over this in much more
detail in the next two videos.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.275
All right, that's enough
about shapes and sizes.

00:04:34.275 --> 00:04:36.150
If you want to read
through the full details,

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.270
check out the link
in the description.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.470
Let's jump to another potential
way to save major costs,

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.250
preemptible VMs.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.750
Typically when
creating a machine,

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.570
you can specify if you want
to make it preemptible or not.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:50.120
And you can pass it along as
you're creating nodes and node

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.770
pools in GKE.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.950
So preemptible means the
machines cost a lot less, even

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:57.460
up to 80% less.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.930
But there's a lot to consider
when using preemptibles.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.570
First of all, any instance
can last a maximum

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.570
of 24 hours and sometimes less.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.980
They can go away with
really short notice.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:13.530
So you need to ask yourselves
are my apps fault-tolerant?

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:17.130
That is to say, what would
happen if a machine goes poof?

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.870
On top of that, what happens
when multiple machines go poof?

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.102
If there's no spare room
to reschedule your app,

00:05:23.102 --> 00:05:24.810
it might take some
time before Kubernetes

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.630
can bring up enough machines
to run your apps again.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.940
Definitely keep in mind
how your apps handle things

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.270
like state, potential
unavailability, and less

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.950
than graceful terminations.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.690
There's a great guide
in the description

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:40.060
that goes over how to architect
with these things in mind.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:41.970
So if you want to
use preemptibles,

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.550
make sure that you can
consider how your cluster can

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.080
work with your apps.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.820
The cluster
autoscaler, which we'll

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.060
talk about the next video, will
actually prefer preemptibles

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.550
because it optimizes for cost.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.620
But you'll probably
want a backup node

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.980
pool that isn't just using
preemptibles for safety.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.140
Just like the type and
shape of your machines,

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.780
you need to know how the full
details of your applications

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.700
work in order to
optimize around them.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.260
Now let's flip
this and talk about

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.655
super predictable workloads.

00:06:10.655 --> 00:06:13.030
If you're planning on keeping
clusters running for years,

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:16.412
you should consider my favorite
committed use discounts.

00:06:16.412 --> 00:06:18.370
These are basically
contracts where you sign up

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.200
for a one or three
year commitment

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.710
on a certain number of
resources, like CPU or memory.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.780
You're committed to
paying for them regardless

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:27.580
of if you actually
end up using them,

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:30.560
but you get a huge
discount in return.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.830
I've got a whole other
video in the description

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:35.230
that goes into more details
about how they work.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.870
For GKE, think about
your resource usage

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:39.520
and how spiky it is.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.380
If it goes up and
down a lot or it

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.450
needs to handle
really large spikes

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:45.220
but there's a
minimum amount that's

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.980
being used no matter what,
you could make a committed use

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:51.310
discount to make that
baseline much cheaper.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.590
Here's a good example where
some usage is covered,

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:56.530
but there's more
room to save money.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.540
And you can even
consider preemptibles

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.180
to handle spiky demand
in a cost-efficient way,

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.250
as long as your app
can tolerate it.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.290
For example, if you're
a retail website,

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:08.770
you may need to handle large
spikes for sales or holidays.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:10.660
On other days, you can
get an idea of what's

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.530
required to serve your website.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.200
So there's a pretty
easy baseline

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.995
to figure out right there.

00:07:15.995 --> 00:07:17.620
Of course, let's not
forget about where

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.350
these different
resources actually exist.

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.210
Regions have different
prices and they'll

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:24.460
offer different
latencies for your users,

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:25.900
based on where they are.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.210
If your application
isn't latency-sensitive,

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:29.770
you can pick a cheaper region.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.340
But keep in mind that moving
data between separate regions

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:35.680
does have a cost
associated with it.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.420
That also affects your clusters.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.970
So a single-zone cluster
probably makes more sense

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:41.470
for a dev environment.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:43.480
And a regional cluster
might make more sense

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:45.280
for a production environment.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.890
You can always run different
workloads in different regions,

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:50.790
based on the unique
requirements of your business.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.020
Inter-pod affinity
and anti-affinity

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.670
are Kubernetes
configurations that you

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:58.480
can use to help control where
your pods will be scheduled.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.760
You have the flexibility
to really customize

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.200
your clusters and machines.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.660
But once again, it comes
down to understanding

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.250
your applications.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.588
On the next video, we'll
dive into auto scaling.

00:08:08.588 --> 00:08:10.630
Remember to check out the
link in the description

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:14.730
for the full guide on
GKE cost optimization.

